db/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500273 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea4e14ee-7928-5c6b-b973-9fa55f6b887b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500276 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500276 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500276 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500276 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500276 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500276 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500276 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500276 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500276 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f56de714-5cdc-5f19-b3b0-cef270a1eb6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500277 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500277 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500277 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500277 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500277 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8252af9-6724-51ce-b683-1b74d5d5cb10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500289 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500289 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500289 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500289 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b6/b6462df728eefef5a942fb409153dc1ba67d4bc065afb281f86b1bd9ac751d20.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $7\\,\\text{mm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $12\\,\\text{mm}$ long?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500289 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17fd30dd-fe66-5909-8dae-293b86cae983" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500289 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $7\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=7.$ Also, when the sides are $12\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $x=12.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(7)\\cdot 2(12)=168{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $168\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500298 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500298 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500298 #edb/ref 1071 "The sides (in inches) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to eleven times the rate of increase of the sides. What is the area of the square at that moment?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500298 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2dde4916-a480-580f-9e20-db5c7a8b1aba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500298 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=11\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 11\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 11 & =2x \\\\ x & =5.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $x=5.5\\text{ in}$ and the area of the square is\n\n$$\nA={5.5}^{2}=30.25{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500310 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500310 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500310 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500310 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$ At what rate is the diagonal of the square decreasing when the diagonal is $20\\text{ cm}$ long?\n\nThe diagonal is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500310 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f2920eb-bbfa-5091-822d-b1cc3cc2b1d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500310 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square decreases at a rate of $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-10.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $20\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $h=20.$ Substituting this information into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.5\\text{ cm}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal is decreasing at $0.5\\text{ cm}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500317 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500317 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500317 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500317 #edb/ref 1071 "The sides (in meters) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to fourteen times the rate of increase of the sides. What is the area $A$ of the square at that moment?\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{ m}}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500317 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b15db96-5f99-5a26-80eb-fe738443b083" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500317 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=14\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 14\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 14 & =2x \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $x=7\\text{ m}$ and the area of the square is\n\n$$\nA={7}^{2}=49{\\text{ m}}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500318 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500318 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500318 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500318 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate of $25{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the lengths of the sides of the square increasing when the sides are $2\\text{ cm}$ long?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500318 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7140d279-862c-5a41-be5a-097813177226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500318 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/a2/a2bd105cbf7aa6fc2af8b2189e63122acfbb0bcf70b007cd35efd8492d273dca.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =25\\text{ cm}{}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{25}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $2\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=2.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{25}{2\\cdot (2)}=\\frac{25}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lengths of the sides are increasing at a rate of $\\frac{25}{4}\\,\\text{cm/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500324 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500324 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500324 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500324 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/19/199f1c7e18eb0c25bf7caafb8e2e71fa3e0165af81083ea57c1fc16a74935ab3.png)\n\nA square's diagonals decrease at the rate of  $2\\text{ m}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square decreasing when the diagonals are $3\\text{ m}$ long each?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500324 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "850324c7-bb98-55b1-99ad-faf0afddb108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500324 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2\\,\\text{m/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=3.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\cdot 3=-6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500325 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500325 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500325 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/97/97a7d465f8412c88367ed3cf148af3ce387441284c10e3f84888c0186fa597ce.png)\n\nPeter wants to cut a piece of square-shaped glass while maintaining its shape. The area of this glass is decreasing at a rate of $1.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$ At what rate are the diagonals of the glass decreasing if the length of these diagonals is $50\\text{ in}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500325 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fede564-db3c-5749-8f57-8bfacce22a82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500325 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $A$ decreases at a rate of $1.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=50.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{50}\\cdot (-1.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1.5}{50} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the diagonals of the glass are decreasing at a rate of $0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500334 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500334 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500334 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ef/ef685549b562c570914bc8b5b5066cb621e42c8536586b37467afc6c1eb6c194.png)\n\nAn adjustable square skylight is being expanded as part of a building renovation. The area of the skylight is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals increasing when each diagonal measures $18\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe diagonal is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2135fd1-33ca-5d7e-9987-278b24125900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500334 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square skylight, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=27.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=18.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 27 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{27}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =1.5\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the skylight is increasing at a rate of \n$1.5\\text{ mm/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500346 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500346 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500346 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500346 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e1/e1fd2786b6a375a4c1d0151b38ad5daf222631ee33e96bc02ada00ef67e051ce.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $6\\text{ m/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the side is $5\\,\\text{m}$ long?\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500346 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bcf0d4e1-9821-591f-a3fd-6de72f482ee3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500346 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{m/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6.$ Also, when the sides are $5\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=5.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\cdot 2(5) \\\\  & =60{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at\n$60\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500353 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500353 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500353 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500353 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e4/e4851331d4899976fb59bb469f19604716fd33235470d24a725abbb26e886279.png)\n\nThe diagonals of a square are decreasing at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate is the perimeter of the square decreasing?\n\nThe perimeter is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500353 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c26c64d2-f1a3-5c4d-8afc-7e98e275ec7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500353 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}\\,h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-7\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}=(-7\\sqrt{2})(2\\sqrt{2})=-28\\text{ m/min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $28\\,\\text{m/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500381 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500381 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500381 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500381 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square is decreasing at a rate of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the side of ​​the square decreasing when the side is $4\\text{ cm}$ long?\n\nThe side is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500381 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8bf196f-ddea-5382-bd16-d659e05ea09d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500381 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/10/10e47419775a585e10e732f482ee279bba49d205ee12959cd80c8d33924accbf.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-48\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-48 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{48}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{48}{2\\cdot 4}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the side is decreasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500382 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500382 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500382 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500382 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/88/88adbcd9d9bd93da5386546b517d765ef6ccd584ae690e7d4ce0d3047cdfc48c.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500382 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb99c2d9-3bf4-5523-9da0-10f469bef9ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500382 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ cm}\\text{/s},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-7\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-7\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-28\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $28\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
